 for your most complex tasks\nL203: \nL204: []\nL205: \nL206: Preferences\nL207: \nL208:   * [Select]\nL209: \nL210: Links\nL211: \nL212:   * cite53†Images L213:   * cite54†RSS feed L214: # Gemini 3.1 Pro: A smarter model for your most complex tasks\nL215: \nL216: Feb 19, 2026\nL217: \nL218: |\nL219: \nL220: 3.1 Pro is designed for tasks where a simple answer isn’t enough.\nL221: \nL222: * * *\nL223: \nL224: Equipe do Google Gemini\nL225: \nL226: * * *\nL227: \nL228: cite55†Image: Gemini 3.1 Pro†storage.googleapis.com L229: \nL230: Your browser does not support the audio element.\nL231: \nL232: Listen to article\nL233: \nL234: [[duration]] minutes\nL235: \nL236: This content is generated by Google AI. Generative AI is experimental\nL237: \nL238: [Input]\nL239: \nL240: Voice Speed\nL241: \nL242: Voice\nL243: \nL244: Speed 0.75X 1X 1.5X 2X\nL245: \nL246: Read AI-generated summary\nL247: Gemini 3.1 Pro is here to help you tackle complex tasks. The upgraded core intelligence is rolling out across consumer and developer products. You can access 3.1 Pro through the Gemini API, Vertex AI, the Gemini app, and NotebookLM.\nL248: \nL249: Summaries were generated by Google AI. Generative AI is experimental.\nL250:   * \"Gemini 3.1 Pro: A smarter model for your most complex tasks\" introduces Google's upgraded AI model.\nL251:   * Gemini 3.1 Pro is rolling out to developers, enterprises, and consumers via various platforms.\nL252:   * This new model shows improved reasoning, scoring significantly higher on complex problem-solving benchmarks.\nL253:   * It's designed for tasks needing advanced reasoning, like synthesizing data or explaining complex topics.\nL254:   * Google is releasing 3.1 Pro in preview to validate updates and advance agentic workflows further.\nL255: Summaries were generated by Google AI. Generative AI is experimental.\nL256: #### Explore other styles:\nL257: \nL258:   * General summary\nL259:   * Bullet points\nL260: \nL261: Last week, we released a major update to cite56†Gemini 3 Deep Think to solve modern challenges across science, research and engineering. Today, we’re releasing the upgraded core intelligence that makes those breakthroughs possible: Gemini 3.1 Pro. We are shipping 3.1 Pro across our consumer and developer products to bring this progress in intelligence to your everyday applications.\nL262: \nL263: Starting today, 3.1 Pro is rolling out:\nL264:   * For developers in preview via the Gemini API in cite57†Google AI Studio†aistudio.google.com , cite58†Gemini CLI†geminicli.com , our agentic development platform cite59†Google Antigravity†antigravity.google and cite60†Android Studio†d.android.com L265:   * For enterprises in cite61†Vertex AI and Gemini Enterprise†cloud.google.com L266:   * For consumers via the Gemini app and NotebookLM\nL267: Building on the Gemini 3 series, 3.1 Pro represents a step forward in core reasoning. 3.1 Pro is a smarter, more capable baseline for complex problem-solving. This is reflected in our progress on rigorous benchmarks. On ARC-AGI-2, a benchmark that evaluates a model’s ability to solve entirely new logic patterns, 3.1 Pro achieved a verified score of 77.1%. This is more than double the reasoning performance of 3 Pro.\nL268: cite62†Image: Side-by-side comparison of different benchmarks for AI models.†storage.googleapis.com L269: ## Intelligence applied\nL270: \nL271: 3.1 Pro is designed for tasks where a simple answer isn’t enough, taking advanced reasoning and making it useful for your hardest challenges. This improved intelligence can help in practical applications — whether you’re looking for a clear, visual explanation of a complex topic, a way to synthesize data into a single view, or bringing a creative project to life.\nL272: Code-based animation: 3.1 Pro can generate website-ready, animated SVGs directly from a text prompt. Because these are built in pure code rather than pixels, they remain crisp at any scale and maintain incredibly small file sizes compared to traditional video.\nL273: Complex system synthesis: 3.1 Pro utilizes advanced reasoning to bridge the gap between complex APIs and user-friendly design. In this example, the model built a live aerospace dashboard, successfully configuring a public telemetry stream to visualize the International Space Station’s orbit.\nL274: Interactive design: 3.1 Pro codes a complex 3D starling murmuration. It doesn't just generate the visual code; it builds an immersive experience where users can manipulate the flock with hand-tracking and listen to a generative score that shifts based on the birds’ movement. For researchers and designers, this provides a powerful way to prototype sensory-rich interfaces.\nL275: Creative coding: 3.1 Pro can translate literary themes into functional code. When prompted to build a modern personal portfolio for Emily Brontë’s \"Wuthering Heights,\" the model didn’t just summarize the text. It reasoned through the novel’s atmospheric tone to design a sleek, contemporary interface, creating a website that captures the essence of the protagonist.\nL276: ## What’s next\nL277: \nL278: Since releasing Gemini 3 Pro in November, your feedback and the pace of progress have driven these rapid improvements. We are releasing 3.1 Pro in preview today to validate these updates and continue to make further advancements in areas such as ambitious agentic workflows before we make it generally available soon.\nL279: Starting today, Gemini 3.1 Pro in the Gemini app is rolling out with higher limits for users with the Google AI Pro and Ultra plans. 3.1 Pro is also now available on NotebookLM exclusively for Pro and Ultra users. And developers and enterprises can access 3.1 Pro now in preview in the Gemini API via AI Studio, Antigravity, Vertex AI, Gemini Enterprise, Gemini CLI and Android Studio.\nL280: \nL281: We can’t wait to see what you build and discover with it.\nL282: \nL283: Posted in:\nL284: \nL285:   * cite63†Gemini models L286:   * Privacy\nL287:   * Terms\nL288:   * Help\nL289:   * [Select]\nL290: \nL291:   * [Select]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQwen (https://qwen.ai/blog)\nciteturn29428view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://qwen.ai/blog\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 0\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nResearch \\ Anthropic (https://www.anthropic.com/research)\nciteturn29428view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.anthropic.com/research\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 58\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content cite1†Skip to footer L1: \nL2:   * Research\nL3:   * cite2†Policy L4:   * Commitments\nL5:   * Learn\nL6:   * cite3†News L7: \nL8: cite4†Try Claude†claude.ai L9: \nL10: # Research\nL11: \nL12: Our research teams investigate the safety, inner workings, and societal impacts of AI models—so that artificial intelligence has a positive impact as it becomes increasingly capable.\nL13: \nL14: Research teams:cite5†Alignment cite6†Economics cite7†Interpretability cite8†Societal Impacts cite9†Frontier Red Team L15: ### Alignment\nL16: \nL17: The Alignment team works to understand the risks of AI models and develop ways to ensure that future ones remain helpful, honest, and harmless.\nL18: \nL19: ### Economics\nL20: \nL21: The Economics team studies how AI is reshaping the economy, including work, productivity, and economic opportunity.\nL22: \nL23: ### Frontier Red Team\nL24: \nL25: The Frontier Red Team analyzes the implications of frontier AI models for cybersecurity, biosecurity, and autonomous systems.\nL26: ### Interpretability\nL27: \nL28: The mission of the Interpretability team is to understand how large language models work internally, as a foundation for AI safety and positive outcomes.\nL29: ### Societal Impacts\nL30: \nL31: Working closely with the Anthropic Policy and Safeguards teams, Societal Impacts is a technical research team that explores how AI is used in the real world.\nL32: \nL33: cite10†Image: Scenarios for our Economic Future L34: cite11†Scenarios for our Economic Future Anthropic’s Economics team is sharing a new model of how AI may affect economic growth, jobs, wages, and more by 2030. The model lets you explore the scenarios, tell us what you think will happen, and see how your answers compare to +10,000 Americans. L35: cite12†ScienceSep 23, 2026 Claude discovers a novel enzyme system with CRISPR-like repeats We’re announcing a new life sciences research group and laboratory at Anthropic.\nL36: This post introduces the team behind this work and shares early results in which Claude discovered a novel enzyme system with properties reminiscent of CRISPR, with only high-level direction from our scientists. cite13†Sep 17, 2026 Measurements for understanding the pace of AI development inside frontier labs Today, the world can’t see what’s going on inside AI labs.\nL37: Anthropic is proposing new metrics that would give the public visibility into frontier AI development. cite14†ScienceSep 4, 2026 Formalizing Fermat's Last Theorem We are sharing the first complete computer-checked proof of Fermat’s Last Theorem.\nL38: Claude worked largely autonomously over 11 days to write the proof in the Lean programming language. cite15†AlignmentSep 9, 2026 An alignment assessment of recent cybersecurity incidents We present an alignment assessment of four incidents in which Claude models gained unauthorized access to real third-party systems. L39: ## Publications\nL40: \nL41: Search[Input: Search]\nL42: \nL43: Date Category Title\nL44:   * cite16†Oct 1, 2026Science Claude-shaped science L45:   * cite17†Sep 30, 2026Economics What work can robots do? L46:   * cite18†Sep 29, 2026Societal Impacts What do you want from AI? L47:   * cite19†Sep 29, 2026Frontier Red Team GLM-5.3 and the spread of advanced cyber capabilities L48:   * cite20†Sep 25, 2026Science Yes, Claude can do Nine Loops L49:   * cite21†Sep 24, 2026Economics Project Swap: What happens when agents trade for us? L50:   * cite22†Sep 17, 2026Science How Claude is uplifting biomolecular modeling L51:   * cite23†Sep 10, 2026Frontier Red Team Measuring tactical intelligence targeting and conventional weapons capabilities of AI models L52:   * cite15†Sep 9, 2026Alignment An alignment assessment of recent cybersecurity incidents L53:   * cite14†Sep 4, 2026Science Formalizing Fermat's Last Theorem L54: cite24†See more L55: Join the Research team\nL56: \nL57: cite25†See open roles "
